## Introduction

Across several cultures, people have developed beautiful ways of drawing continuous curves around grids of dots. In Tamil Nadu, women create *kolams* on the thresholds of their homes each morning, using rice flour to draw lines that loop around a set of dots called *pulli* in Tamil. In eastern Angola and neighbouring regions, the Tchokwe people draw *sona* (singular *lusona*) in the sand while telling stories; these drawings also embrace a rectangular array of dots, preferably with a single closed line. Similar ideas appear in various *rangoli* traditions across India.

What is striking is how often these drawings are ***monolinear*** (made of one continuous closed curve) and how frequently they display strong symmetry. Paulus Gerdes, working in the tradition of ethnomathematics, showed that a large and important class of these designs can be understood through a simple **physical metaphor**: imagine the outer boundary of the rectangle is mirrored, and that we place additional double-sided mirrors in the spaces between the dots. A ray of light that enters at 45 degrees will bounce around and eventually close on itself, tracing out exactly the kind of curve found in kolams and lusona. The same construction also produces the elegant black-and-white patterns Gerdes named *Lunda designs*.

In this module we introduce one ordinary matrix — we will call it the **Mirror Indicator Matrix** — that lets us hold all of these ideas at once. The entries of the matrix tell us where the mirrors go. Those same entries describe a network (a graph) on which the light ray is simply a ***walk***. And because the matrix is just a table of numbers, the symmetries we already understand ~~from linear algebra~~ become design tools: if the numbers are symmetric in a certain way, the finished drawing will be symmetric in the corresponding geometric way. You can change a few entries and, almost without pause, obtain both a new network and a new `kolam` or `lusona`.

This module is designed to sit alongside the existing course page on [Kolams and Lusona](../60-kolams/index.qmd). The code below follows the same geometric convention shown in the figures there: the ray travels *between* the pulli at 45°, and the mirrors sit midway between adjacent dots.

## The Mirror Indicator Matrix

Imagine we have decided on a rectangular grid of dots with \( r \) rows and \( c \) columns. Between these dots there are natural places where a short double-sided mirror can sit:

- A vertical mirror can be placed in the gap between two dots that are neighbours left and right.
- A horizontal mirror can be placed in the gap between two dots that are neighbours above and below.
- CHECK: Why not the other way around too?

For teaching purposes we gather all of this information into a single matrix \( M \) whose dimensions are roughly those of the grid itself. Each entry is a small whole number that records what (if anything) is present at that location:

| Entry | Meaning              | What it does to a path                  | Quick symbol |
|-------|----------------------|-----------------------------------------|--------------|
| 0     | nothing              | the path may continue straight          | ·            |
| 1     | vertical mirror      | forces the path to bounce left or right | \|           |
| −1    | horizontal mirror    | forces the path to bounce up or down    | −            |
| 2     | both (a crossing)    | both kinds of reflection are present    | +            |

Here is a small concrete example for a 2-by-2 array of dots:

$$
M = \begin{bmatrix}
O & - & O \\
1 &  & 1 \\
O & - & O
\end{bmatrix}
$$


According to the table above, every $1$ means a **vertical** mirror and every $−$ means a **horizontal** mirror. The matrix here contains both, so it places four mirrors: one H-mirror in the middle of the top row, one H-mirror in the middle of the bottom row, and two V-mirrors on each side of the middle row. The four mirrors form a kind of cross when you look at their positions. The light-ray path that results is still a single closed curve embracing all nine dots. Because the pattern of 1s looks the same after a 90° rotation of the matrix, the drawing inherits a corresponding rotational symmetry. The symmetry is already visible in the numbers; we do not have to impose it afterwards on the curve.

